I've got what is genuinely good news  about net neutrality if not monumental  news which is that at the state level  Washington State has approved their own  net neutrality requirements which sounds  really great and it's good but I don't  want to pretend like this is some  panacea Washington's the first state to  actually enact net neutrality  requirements since the FCC repealed the  Obama era net neutrality regulations  many states are discussing them some  states have done executive orders I  believe Oregon has agreed to some laws  but have have not yet enacted them so  what this will do is it will stop any  state agencies in Washington state from  doing business with ISPs that block  prioritize or throttle particular  internet content or apps and it'll also  force ISPs operating in Washington to  disclose the management practices that  they're employing there what this does  not do is mandate that ISPs at large  can't throttle or prioritize Internet  traffic or content that really would be  the big thing right but listen it's  something and there are lots of states  looking at doing similar things and we  should be honestly at this point glad  for anything that can be done other  states that are looking at similar rules  include Hawaii New Jersey New York  Montana and Vermont but I think that  there's actually sort of a bigger fish  to fry here  we often talk on this program about when  does the free market work and when  doesn't the free market work and when it  comes to something like healthcare I've  said it should not be in the so-called  free or open market we should eliminate  the profit motive from providing health  care to people because the demand for  health care is completely inelastic and  it just simply should not be operating  under a free market when it comes to  Internet service providers though part  of the problem is and part of why  Internet service providers treat  customers so terribly and regularly are  the most hated companies nationally is  that they offer terrible service at a  high price because there is no  competition or almost no competition and  if states instead of passing executive  orders that just say we will only deal  with  isps as a customer as a state customer  that don't do this or don't do that if  instead they tried to deal with the fact  that we have almost no legislation to  prevent these two facto monopolies that  exist in most places around the country  you would very quickly see that the need  for net neutrality rules goes way down  I'm not saying that if we did the  dealing of with the monopolies we can  just forget about net neutrality I think  we should have both but if you actually  get these ISPs competing you were going  to have a drastically different  situation not going to happen anytime  soon these are deliberately constructed  monopolies by design and that's because  an isp will come in and I admit there's  a lot of spending that has to be done to  establish the infrastructure for  high-speed internet and cable in a city  but the ISP will come in and I'll say  well we will set up shop in your city  town municipality whatever but you need  to commit to only doing business with us  and it's a real problem for bigger  cities we've seen small places there's  hill towns out in western Massachusetts  that have done this I think Leverett and  shoots Barry who are small enough that  they can do municipal broadband but that  is not the majority of places a lot of  cities especially cities that  financially are not doing well just  can't afford to do that and that's  something that needs to be dealt with  Google Fiber can can sort of help to  deal with it although development has  really slowed down maybe something like  Elon Musk satellite internet could help  but the states are gonna need to deal  with that the cities maybe if we really  want to have a shot at fixing this thing  we have a fantastic bonus show for you  today I'm out of time but trust me it's  a good one no voice mail today either  because I was ranting about net  neutrality and monopolies we've got a  great bonus show for you and we'll talk  to you on the program tomorrow as well
 

